## corpus/conc/proc_must_join_legal.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="child sees: built in pass, 7 uses 1\nchild sees: built in pass, 7 uses 2\nchild sees: built in pass, 7 uses 3\nchild sees: built in pass, 7 uses 4\nchild sees: built in pass, 7 uses 5\nchild sees: built in pass, 7 uses 6\n1 1 3 9 7")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conc.proc.arg, conc.proc.join, mem.region.escape
//!
//! s233 (wolf-lang#678's helper half): the sound neighbours of the
//! must-join check. Each helper is handed bytes built in `main`'s
//! region block and spawns a proc with them, and every path out of it
//! waits for the proc first — a join, a deferred join an early return
//! runs, a join through a copy of the handle, a join in each
//! iteration, a join on both arms of an `if`. So the proc never reads
//! after `pass` is freed, and every call stays legal. A helper that
//! may return with its proc still running is E1010 at the call
//! (`[conc.proc.arg]`); those shapes run on lupin, so they are the
//! gate `proc_must_join_lanes`'s fixtures, not corpus rows.
## corpus/os/ids_read.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="ids: true\ngroups: true\nowner of a new file is the effective user: true")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.proc.ids, err.union
//!
//! s236 (wolf-lang#633, `[os.proc.ids]`) — who this process runs as,
//! with one truth on every tier-1 host. The real and effective user
//! and group ids are each a number from 0 where the host has such ids,
//! and `unsupported`, by name, where it has none (windows) — all four
//! alike, and the supplementary groups with them. A file the process
//! creates is owned by its EFFECTIVE user (`fs_stat`'s word 6), which
//! is the one fact about the ids a program can check without being
//! told who it is. The numbers themselves are the host's: the gate
//! `crates/wolf_driver/tests/ids_lanes.rs` holds them to `id`. lupin
//! has no host call for an id and refuses `os_uid` by name.
## corpus/os/ids_rows.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="set_ids(uid -1)=invalid set_ids(gid 4294967295)=invalid set_ids(group -1)=invalid\nset_owner(uid -2)=invalid set_owner(gid 4294967295)=invalid set_owner_fd(uid -2)=invalid\nos_error=0\nleave=true same=true by_handle=true\nmissing=true forged=true\nunchanged=true cleaned=true")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.proc.ids.set, os.fs.owner, os.fs.error, err.union
//!
//! s236 (wolf-lang#701, #742) — the rows of the id and owner calls that
//! change nothing, one truth on every host. Each call judges its
//! arguments before it asks the host anything: a user or group id is
//! 0..=4294967294 (the all-ones word is every host's "leave this one
//! alone", so it is never an id), an owner word may also be -1
//! ("leave"), and `os_set_ids` has no "leave" at all — each of these is
//! `invalid`, on windows too, where the well-formed call would be
//! `unsupported`. None reaches the host, so `os_error()` stays 0.
//! Then the owner calls that are well formed and change nothing: -1 -1,
//! and the owner the file already has (`fs_stat`'s words 6 and 7), by
//! path and on a handle — `ok` where the host has owners,
//! `unsupported` where it has none (windows: `fs_stat` answers no
//! owner there, its `have` bit clear). A missing path is `not_found`
//! and a forged handle `io`. The rows that DO change an id or an owner
//! run inside a throwaway container, as the super-user:
//! `crates/wolf_driver/tests/ids_lanes.rs`.
## corpus/os/term_editor_rows.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="wait_empty=io\nwait_send_only=io\nwait_past_table=io\nmode_bit5=invalid\nmode_negative=invalid\nmode_past_vtime=invalid")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.signal.wait.fd, os.signal.set, os.term.mode, err.union
//!
//! s234 (wolf-lang#719, W05; pelt's line editor) — the rows of the
//! terminal surface that need no terminal and wait for nothing, one truth
//! on every host. The wait on a descriptor and a signal set takes the one
//! shape rule every set-taking call does (`[os.signal.set]`): an empty
//! set, a send-only meaning (`KILL`, 1048576) and a bit past the table
//! are each `io` before the handle is looked at — on windows too. A mode
//! is `invalid` before its handle is read for a bit that is no field —
//! bit 5 (32) now that `FLOW` (8) and `EXTENDED` (16) are fields — a
//! negative mode, or a bit past `VTIME`. The live witnesses (the size, a
//! resize while waiting, a key and a signal ending one wait, the special
//! characters and the two flags) run under a pseudo-terminal in
//! `crates/wolf_driver/tests/term_editor_lanes.rs` (unix).
