+The table is a measurement of wolf 0.2.15, and its first row is not true
+of the compiler this book is printed with. From wolf 0.2.27 the native
+root arena gives each thread its own window to allocate from and takes
+its lock only to refill one, once per 64 KiB (`[conc.task.par.cost]`),
+so an `f` that allocates on every call no longer queues on the root. The
+same three programs, built by both compilers and timed on a second
+machine on 16 October 2026 (Linux on x86-64, 16 cores; the median of
+seven runs; the first program exactly as printed, the other two rebuilt
+from the description above):
+
+| program | wolf 0.2.15 serial | `par` | wolf 0.2.30 serial | `par` |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| the word counter as printed above | 0.27 s | 0.90 s | 0.26 s | 0.03 s |
+| one allocation per word removed | 0.14 s | 0.02 s | 0.14 s | 0.02 s |
+| a loop that computes and allocates nothing | 1.53 s | 0.15 s | 1.52 s | 0.15 s |
+
+On the older compiler the printed counter is 3.3x slower with `par`, and
+its parallel run spends 8.41 s inside the kernel. On the newer one it is
+more than eight times faster and spends 0.02 s there. Nothing in the program
+changed. The two rows below it did not move at all, because neither
+allocates enough to have queued.
+
 The specification says this in one sentence, and it is the sentence to
